# The Indus Valley Civilization: Bronze Age Urban Genius

> South Asia's first urban culture flourished from about 2600 to 1900 BCE
> on the floodplains of the Indus and the now-dry Ghaggar-Hakra. Larger in
> area than Egypt and Mesopotamia combined, it is remembered less for kings
> than for grid streets, covered drains, and standardised bricks.

## 1. Discovery and Dating

The civilization surfaced only in the 1920s, when the Archaeological Survey
of India excavated mounds in Punjab and Sindh and its Director-General,
**John Marshall**, announced the finds in 1924.

| Phase | Period | Character |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Early Harappan | c. 3300-2600 BCE | Villages growing into towns |
| Mature Harappan | c. 2600-1900 BCE | Cities, seals, and script in full bloom |
| Late Harappan | c. 1900-1300 BCE | Cities decay; regional rural cultures |

| Site | Year | Excavator | River / Region |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Harappa | 1921 | Daya Ram Sahni | Ravi, Punjab (Pakistan) |
| Mohenjo-daro | 1922 | R. D. Banerji | Indus, Sindh |
| Lothal | 1954-55 | S. R. Rao | Bhogavo, Gujarat |
| Kalibangan | 1961 | B. B. Lal, B. K. Thapar | Ghaggar, Rajasthan |
| Ropar (Rupnagar) | 1953 | Y. D. Sharma | Sutlej, Punjab |
| Dholavira | 1990s | R. S. Bisht | Khadir island, Kutch |
| Rakhigarhi | from 1997 | A. Nath, V. Shinde | Ghaggar, Haryana |

[info title="Why the two names?"]
"Indus Valley Civilization" follows the first-discovered river valley;
"Harappan" is preferred because most of the thousand-plus known sites lie
beyond the Indus — about a third along the Ghaggar-Hakra system.
[/info]

## 2. Extent and Major Sites

The Mature Harappan world stretched roughly 1,100 km east-west and 1,600 km
north-south — the largest territory of the three great Bronze Age
civilizations.

@diagram tree id="ivc-sites" layout="tree" aria="Major Harappan sites grouped by river region"
  Harappan Sites -> "Indus Valley"
  Harappan Sites -> "Ghaggar-Hakra Plains"
  Harappan Sites -> "Gujarat and the East"
  Indus Valley -> "Mohenjo-daro (Sindh)"
  Indus Valley -> "Harappa (Punjab)"
  Indus Valley -> "Chanhudaro (Sindh)"
  Ghaggar-Hakra Plains -> "Kalibangan (Rajasthan)"
  Ghaggar-Hakra Plains -> "Rakhigarhi (Haryana)"
  Gujarat and the East -> "Dholavira (Kutch)"
  Gujarat and the East -> "Lothal (dockyard)"
@/diagram

Border sites fix the map: **Sutkagendor** (west, Baluchistan),
**Alamgirpur** (east, Uttar Pradesh), **Manda** (north, Jammu), and
**Daimabad** (south, Maharashtra, with its bronze chariot). **Rakhigarhi**
in Haryana is now rated the largest Harappan site.

## 3. Town Planning and Sanitation

- **Grid plan:** streets ran at right angles; house doors opened on side
  lanes, never on the main street.
- **Two zones:** a raised **citadel** (west) and a **lower town** (east);
  **Dholavira** uniquely kept three divisions, ringed by vast reservoirs.
- **Baked bricks** in a fixed ratio of 1 : 2 : 4, standard from Harappa
  to Lothal.
- **Drainage:** covered drains with soak pits and manholes ran below every
  street, connected to house drains.

[definition]
A **citadel** is a raised, fortified upper town holding public buildings —
baths, granaries, assembly halls — separate from the crowded lower town
where most people lived.
[/definition]

The most famous citadel structure is Mohenjo-daro's **Great Bath**: a
sunken tank 11.88 m by 7.01 m and 2.43 m deep, waterproofed with bitumen —
likely a ritual complex. Both great cities had granary rows with
threshing platforms.

## 4. Economy, Weights, and Trade

- **Agriculture:** wheat and barley staples; sesame, peas, and the Old
  World's first **cotton** — Mesopotamians called the cloth *sindon*.
- **Weights:** chert cubes in a **binary series** — 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 — with
  larger units in decimal multiples such as 160 and 320.
- **Seals** of steatite stamped goods; the one-horned "unicorn" appears on
  about sixty per cent of them.
- **Foreign trade:** Sumerian texts of Sargon of Akkad (c. 2370 BCE) mention
  ships from **Meluhha**, the Indus land; Harappan seals, carnelian beads,
  and weights occur at Ur, Kish, and Susa, via **Dilmun** (Bahrain) and
  **Magan** (Oman).
- **Lothal's** brick basin, 216 m by 37 m, is read as a **dockyard**,
  beside its bead factory and terracotta toy ship.

[example]
Binary weights let a merchant weigh 3 units with two stones — a 1-weight
and a 2-weight — since doubling builds every value: the same logic as
modern binary arithmetic.
[/example]

## 5. Art, Craft, and Religion

The bronze **Dancing Girl** of Mohenjo-daro (about 10.5 cm, lost-wax
casting) and the trefoil-robed **"priest-king"** bust show confident
portraiture; **Kalibangan** yielded the world's earliest attested ploughed
field plus rows of fire altars. Religion is read from objects: a
**mother goddess** in terracotta, peepal leaves on pots, and the
**Pashupati Seal** — a yogic figure among elephant, tiger, rhino, and
buffalo — read by Marshall as a proto-Shiva. Grand temples, royal tombs,
and weapons of war are conspicuously absent.

## 6. The Undeciphered Script

The script remains unread: roughly 400-600 signs, five or six to a seal,
written right to left (the longest runs to about 26 signs). Decipherment
stalls because no bilingual text exists, the language family is unknown,
and the corpus is tiny.

[warning]
Claims that the script records early Sanskrit — or Dravidian — remain
hypotheses, including the famous attempts of S. R. Rao and Asko Parpola.
Until a bilingual surfaces, every "decipherment" is speculation.
[/warning]

## 7. The Decline Debate

By about 1900 BCE the cities were emptying: drains clogged, brick-robbing
began, settlement drifted east.

| Theory | Chief evidence | Status |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Aryan invasion (M. Wheeler) | Mohenjo-daro skeletons | Now doubted; bones span decades |
| Climate change | Weakening monsoon after c. 2200 BCE | Strongly supported |
| River shifts | Ghaggar-Hakra drying; Sutlej-Yamuna changing course | Supported |
| Floods, waterlogging | Silt layers at Mohenjo-daro | Local only |
| Trade collapse | Mesopotamian demand falling after 2000 BCE | Contributory |

[note]
The Late Harappan was ruralisation, not extinction: Cemetery H at Harappa,
Jhukar in Sindh, and Rangpur in Gujarat carried the culture's crafts into
the second millennium BCE.
[/note]

## 8. Quick Revision

| Item | Fact |
| --- | --- |
| Mature phase | c. 2600-1900 BCE |
| Largest site | Rakhigarhi (Haryana) |
| Great Bath | Mohenjo-daro, 11.88 x 7.01 m |
| Brick ratio | 1 : 2 : 4 |
| Weights | Binary (1, 2, 4, 8, 16 ...) |
| Ploughed field | Kalibangan (Rajasthan) |
| Dockyard | Lothal (Gujarat) |
| Mesopotamian name | Meluhha |
| Water management | Dholavira reservoirs (UNESCO, 2022) |

## Key Takeaways

- The Harappan world was the largest Bronze Age urban culture, run on civic
  discipline rather than visible kings.
- Standardised bricks, binary weights, and one script from Sindh to Kutch
  imply a shared administrative network.
- The undeciphered script keeps its names, language, and rulers unknown.
- Decline around 1900 BCE is now credited mainly to climate and river
  changes, not invasion.
